
  [1mMy recommendation: the correlation id.[0m It's the only one that actually delivers "track who logged in" with the data
  the app has, it keeps the signature honest (the param means something the other arguments don't), and it survives a
  real backend — the server can join its own user id to your correlation id later.

[38;5;246m✻[39m [38;5;246mCooked for 55s · done 3:56 PM[39m

[38;5;239m[48;5;237m❯ [38;5;231mIt should identify the actual user, not just the attempt. It should persist, and other forms will need it later.[39m

[38;5;231m[49m⏺[39m That changes the classification. A persistent identifier for the real user, read by forms that don't exist yet, isn't
  a parameter — it's an identity store with a lifecycle, a storage location, and consumers beyond [38;5;153mlogin[39m. The
  brainstorming ratchet is one-way, so I'm stepping up.

  [1mUpgrading: bounded → architectural.[0m That means questions first, then 2-3 approaches with trade-offs, then a sectioned
  design, then a written spec at [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers/specs/[39m for you to review before any code. Nothing gets implemented
  until you approve.

  Flagging one consequence up front, since it's the kind that's expensive to reverse: a persistent, user-linked
  identifier written to the browser and attached to other forms is a tracking surface. It pulls in consent, retention,
  and "what happens on logout" — and if it ever leaves the browser it's personal data under GDPR/CCPA. I'm not raising
  that to slow you down; it's a design input, and it's much cheaper to decide now than to retrofit.

  First question, the one everything else hangs off:

  [1mThe fork:[0m "the actual user" needs an authoritative source, and this app has none — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is a stub with no backend,
  and the only user-supplied data is a typed username and password. Where the id originates determines what you can
  store, what it's worth, and what a later real backend does to it.

  - [1mServer-issued after auth[0m — [38;5;153mlogin[39m returns a [38;5;153muserId[39m; we persist it and later forms read it. This is the only option
    that genuinely identifies the account, and it's what a real backend will do anyway. Cost: the contract doesn't exist
    yet, so today it's a stub you'd swap out. Nothing pre-login has an id.
  - [1mAnonymous local id, upgraded at login[0m — mint a UUID on first visit, persist it, and link it to the server's [38;5;153muserId[39m
    once auth succeeds. Buys continuity across forms filled [3mbefore[0m login, plus a clean join key. Cost: two ids and a
    linking step — the most moving parts of the three.
  - [1mLocal-only persistent id[0m — a UUID in storage, no backend involvement. Works today with no contract. Cost: it

